    "record_display_title": "Untitled Source",
    "display_title": "Machine Learning Makes Compiler Optimization Empirical",
    "authors": [
      "Zheng Wang",
      "Michael O’Boyle"
    ]
  },
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Machine-learning-based compilation can use a model inside the compiler to choose compiler heuristics or optimizations for a given program according to learned evidence about what makes code faster, smaller, or less power hungry.",
      "short_title": "Learning guides compiler choices",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Given a program, compiler writers would like to know what compiler heuristic or optimisation to apply in order to make the code better. Better often means execute faster, but can also mean smaller code footprint or reduced power. Machine learning can be used to build a model used within the compiler, that makes such decisions for any given program."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The machine-learning compiler workflow separates model learning from deployment: training data is used to learn a model, and the learned model is then inserted into the compiler to make predictions for new programs.",
      "short_title": "Training precedes deployment",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "There are two main stages involved: learning and deployment. The first stage learns the model based on training data, while the second uses the model on new unseen programs.",
        "In the final step, the learned model is inserted into the compiler to predict the best optimisation decisions for new programs."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Program features provide the quantifiable representation that allows learning algorithms to reason about source code, intermediate representation, runtime profiles, or combinations of those properties.",
      "short_title": "Features encode program behavior",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Machine learning relies on a set of quantifiable properties, or features, to characterise the programs. There are many different features that can be used. These include the static data structures extracted from the program source code or the compiler intermediate representation ... dynamic profiling information ... or a combination of the both."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Supervised learning for compiler optimization learns correlations between program-feature values and the optimization decisions that produce optimal or near-optimal empirical performance.",
      "short_title": "Supervision maps features to decisions",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Using supervised machine learning, a predictive model is trained on empirical performance data (labelled outputs) and important quantifiable properties (features) of representative programs. The model learns the correlation between these feature values and the optimisation decision that delivers the optimal (or nearly optimal) performance."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Thread coarsening decisions are context dependent because coarsening can improve instruction-level parallelism and memory behavior while also reducing parallelism or increasing register pressure on particular programs and hardware.",
      "short_title": "Thread coarsening is context dependent",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Thread coarsening can improve performance through increasing instruction-level parallelism, reducing the number of memory-access operations, and eliminating redundant computation when the same value is computed in every work-item. However, it can also have several negative side-effects such as reducing the total amount of parallelism and increasing the register pressure, which can lead to slowdown performance."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Feature selection and dimension reduction are necessary in compiler-learning problems because larger feature spaces require more training examples and can contain redundant information.",
      "short_title": "Feature reduction preserves signal",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "As the number of features increase so does the number of training examples needed to build an accurate model. Hence, we need to limit the dimension of the feature space. In compiler research, commonly, an initial large, high dimensional candidate feature space is pruned via feature selection, or projected into a lower dimensional space.",
        "Principal Component Analysis ... is applied to map the 17 candidate features into 7 aggregated features, so that each aggregated feature is a linear combination of the original features."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Direct prediction of the best compiler option is practical for relatively small optimization problems and has produced measurable gains in loop unrolling and thread-coarsening studies.",
      "short_title": "Constrained choices enable direct prediction",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Stephenson and Amarasinghe ... used over 2,500 loops from 72 benchmarks to train two machine learning models ... their techniques correctly predict the unroll factor for 65% of the testing loops, leading to on average, a 5% improvement for the SPEC 2000 benchmark suite.",
        "These papers show that supervised machine learning can be a powerful tool for modelling problems with a relatively small number of optimisation options."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Machine-learning-based compiler optimization is limited by the quality, representativeness, and cost of its training data and cannot by itself create legal transformations or derive legality analyses.",
      "short_title": "Training data limits learning",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Machine learning can only learn form what is provided by the compiler writer. Machine learning can not invent new program transformations to apply nor can it derive analysis that determines whether or not a transformation is legal – all of this is beyond its scope.",
        "The most immediate problem continues to be gathering enough sufficient high quality training data."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Machine-learning-based compilation can make compiler optimization more evidence based because models must be grounded in empirical data that can be tested and independently scrutinized.",
      "short_title": "Empirical workflows improve compiler evidence",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "One of the real benefits of machine learning based approaches is that it forces an empirical driven approach to compiler construction. New models have to be based on empirical data which can then be verified by independent experimentation.",
        "This paper has introduced machine learning based compilation and described its power in determining an evidence based approach to compiler optimisation."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Future research in machine-learning compilation extends beyond choosing optimizations to mapping program and hardware spaces, learning analyses, discovering common features, and possibly learning better intermediate representations.",
      "short_title": "Open questions extend beyond optimization",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "The open research directions go beyond predicting the best optimisations to apply. One central issue is what does the program space look like?",
        "Can machine learning also be applied to compiler analysis? For instance is it possible to learn dataflow or point-to analysis? ... Can we learn the ideal compiler intermediate representation?"
      ]
    }
  ],
